(The first step in the education of the speculator should be to clear) Tj T*
(away the illusions which have grown rank through ignorance, and) Tj T*
(flourished through prejudice. We have heard, and continue to hear, a) Tj T*
(great deal of ethical talk on this subject, most of which emanates from) Tj T*
(people who are not authorities, and who have little real conception) Tj T*
(of the subject. It would be pretty safe to assume that a majority of) Tj T*
(these same instructors speculate themselves. They place an arbitrary) Tj T*
(construction on the word however, and draw a dividing line between) Tj T*
(stock or cereal operations, and other forms of speculation, although) Tj T*
(the basic principle is the same in all cases, i.e.: to buy what is) Tj T*
(cheap and re-sell at a profit. One of the most ridiculous forms which) Tj T*
(this pedantry assumes is the warning and preaching against speculation) Tj T*
(by very rich men who made their own money speculating and could not) Tj T*
(possibly have acquired it in any other way. Such expressions of opinion) Tj T*
(are born largely of an exaggerated ego.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The trouble with these critics and advisers is that they seldom) Tj T*
(approach the subject in the right way. With a full knowledge of) Tj T*
(the fact that speculation is an inherent part of human nature, and) Tj T*
(that a majority of human beings are bound to indulge in it in spite) Tj T*
(of everything, these sophists simply rail against the practice) Tj T*
(indiscriminately instead of attempting to point out what is foolish) Tj T*
(and fallacious. If we attack the practice in a general way little) Tj T*
(will be accomplished. If we say, ?do not speculate,? our audience) Tj T*
(will perchance give us a respectful hearing,--and go on speculating.) Tj T*
(If, however, we point out what is dangerous and unreasonable, confine) Tj T*
(ourselves to attacking the evils and explaining the delusions,) Tj T*
(some good may be done in an educational way. We may, if we show by) Tj T*
(simple logic that the education and qualifications necessary to) Tj T*
(success are too difficult to acquire, actually deter many people) Tj T*
(from speculating in certain lines at all, a thing which could not) Tj T*
(possibly be accomplished by mere blanket warnings against the practice.) Tj T*
(One of the most serious blunders in the world is the common one of) Tj T*
(under-estimating other people?s intelligence. People are ready and) Tj T*
(willing to learn, and that they do learn is shown by the decreasing) Tj T*
