(No one will attempt to deny that a majority of public speculators) Tj T*
(lose. In a former volume, the present writer undertook to establish) Tj T*
(by analysis of a large number of public accounts, the fact that 80%) Tj T*
(of the participators lost money. A number of critics commented on this) Tj T*
(statement as a body blow to speculation, asserting that the writer) Tj T*
(had shown that there was ?80% against the player.? These writers) Tj T*
(proceeded to compare this percentage with that existing in games of) Tj T*
(pure chance, such as roulette, faro, etc., and wound up by pointing out) Tj T*
(the tremendous drawback to the speculator through percentage against) Tj T*
(the player. It seems incredible that any sane man should fall into such) Tj T*
(laughable confusion of ideas. The percentage of players who lose in any) Tj T*
(game has nothing to do with the percentage against the player. If these) Tj T*
(critics established anything at all, it was that speculation was not) Tj T*
(gambling; for it requires only a moment?s reflection to see that in any) Tj T*
(mechanical gambling game where there is _any_ percentage, no matter how) Tj T*
(small, in favor of the game, the percentage of players who eventually) Tj T*
(lose must be 100. This being the case, the gentlemen mentioned were at) Tj T*
(considerable pains to prove that, as 100 per cent. of the players did) Tj T*
(not lose, speculation was not a gambling game in the strict sense of) Tj T*
(the word. That is to say, it could not be correctly compared with any) Tj T*
(mechanical device where the element of skill was absent.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
