(There is no doubt that fluctuations in prices of securities, cereals) Tj T*
(and staples are frequently used as a basis for mere gambling) Tj T*
(transactions. But the most remarkable feature of the whole problem is) Tj T*
(the fact that the percentage of loss in transactions is _greater_ than) Tj T*
(the mechanical percentage. In the work already mentioned, the writer) Tj T*
(undertook to establish this. In 500 accounts examined, there was a loss) Tj T*
(of $1,245,000, and profits of $288,000, leaving a deficit of $957,000.) Tj T*
(The commission charges and interest amounted to only $275,000. There) Tj T*
(thus appeared a loss of $682,000 which could not be attributed to a) Tj T*
(gambling percentage. It may be added that the period considered in the) Tj T*
(computations was from July, 1901, to March, 1903, and that the price of) Tj T*
(the stock considered \(U. S. Steel Common\) was the same at the beginning) Tj T*
(and the end of the period.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This tends to again refute the theory of mere gambling, with a ruinous) Tj T*
(percentage against the player, for no mechanical device could by any) Tj T*
(possibility operate against the player to a greater extent than the) Tj T*
(fixed percentage in favor of the machine. A gambling machine will stick) Tj T*
(to its knitting. If, for example, we take the simplest form of gambling) Tj T*
(device--two dice thrown from a cup,--we know that certain numbers) Tj T*
(formed by adding the total spots which appear uppermost will show more) Tj T*
(frequently than others. Thus the number two can be effected in but one) Tj T*
(way, the number three in two ways, the number four in three ways, and) Tj T*
(so on up to the number seven, which can be formed by six different) Tj T*
(combinations, thus:) Tj T*
