(One of the most flagrant errors in speculation is an entirely mistaken) Tj T*
(idea as to the _possibilities_ in this field. Nine men out of ten have) Tj T*
(a deep-rooted conviction that if any individual could be right in his) Tj T*
(main deductions for, say one or two years, he should make millions on) Tj T*
(a small capital. This is a great mistake, and leads to numerous minor) Tj T*
(errors which are productive of much loss in actual operations. The) Tj T*
(business of speculation never did, and never will result in abnormal) Tj T*
(profits. Large returns are sometimes made, it is true, but this fact) Tj T*
(is also true of every other line of business. Certain individuals) Tj T*
(grow very rich in Wall Street; this again is true of every commercial) Tj T*
(branch. We hear now and then of a million dollar coup by a Morgan or a) Tj T*
(Rockefeller, and do not stop to consider the great capital behind it.) Tj T*
(If an individual makes five thousand dollars in a year?s speculative) Tj T*
(ventures on a capital of twenty thousand, he is not considered a) Tj T*
(Napoleon of finance, but he has accomplished much more, in proportion) Tj T*
(to his capital, than Rockefeller would have accomplished if he had made) Tj T*
(five millions on similar operations.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In a recent conversation with a number of gentlemen who clung) Tj T*
(tenaciously to this idea of sudden riches, the writer undertook to) Tj T*
(establish his contention. Tapes were secured recording the fluctuations) Tj T*
(of sugar stock during a twenty point decline. The skeptics were given a) Tj T*
(hypothetical capital of $10,000 each, subjected to the ordinary rules) Tj T*
(of trading as to margins, etc., informed that sugar would decline) Tj T*
(twenty points before it again touched the first quotation established,) Tj T*
(and invited to ?get rich quick.? The result was ridiculous in the) Tj T*
(extreme. Two of the experimenters lost their imaginary capital trying) Tj T*
(to double up and show large returns. The third took an unfair stand,) Tj T*
(by selling the maximum amount at the inception of the experiment and) Tj T*
(closing it after the 20 point decline had appeared. His operations,) Tj T*
(therefore, proved nothing. Here was a case where two traders, possessed) Tj T*
(of an absolute fore-knowledge of what was to occur, lost everything) Tj T*
(through the fault of over-speculation and the belief that abnormal) Tj T*
(returns could be made if the ultimate fate of a market could be) Tj T*
(correctly forecasted. Even if we assume that _every_ intermediate) Tj T*
(_movement_ were known in advance, that after a ten point decline there) Tj T*
(would be a five point advance, and that transactions were conducted to) Tj T*
(the full possibilities of both original margin and accrued profits, the) Tj T*
(result would not be the millions which dazzle the eyes and imagination) Tj T*
(of the unsophisticated. But to assume any such trading is foolish. The) Tj T*
(factor of safety would be wholly absent. No wise man will ever attempt) Tj T*
(pyramiding, and no foolish man who does, will succeed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In order to clear the ground for discussion or study, the first thing) Tj T*
(to eliminate is this wholly unsupported and mistaken idea of sudden) Tj T*
(riches. No matter how correct the forecast of the future may be,) Tj T*
(safety disappears in inverse ratio to the increased possibilities of) Tj T*
(abnormal returns; and with the factor of safety continually ignored,) Tj T*
(the final results are bound to be disastrous.) Tj T*
