(_How to Compute the Value of Rights._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?Inasmuch as the method of computing the value of rights is slightly) Tj T*
(complicated, an illustration may be given. Let us take the instance) Tj T*
(of St. Paul again, where the stockholders were allowed to subscribe) Tj T*
(to 23% of their holdings to new stock at par. The common stock was at) Tj T*
(that time selling a little below $200 per share. Let us take the round) Tj T*
(figure, and the operation is as follows:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    One hundred shares at $200 per share equals    $20,000) Tj T*
(    Twenty-three shares at $100 equals               2,300) Tj T*
(                                                   -------) Tj T*
(    Total cost of 123 shares                       $22,300) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?Average cost, $181 per share.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?Deducting $181 from the market quotation leaves $19, the value of) Tj T*
(the rights on each share of St. Paul stock. As a matter of fact, the) Tj T*
(selling price was a little below $200, and the highest price of the) Tj T*
(rights fell a little below $19 per share.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?In other words the process is simply to take the number of new shares) Tj T*
(per hundred shares of the original holding to be subscribed for, and) Tj T*
(add the value of these new shares at the subscription price to the cost) Tj T*
(of one hundred shares at the market price; then divide the total cost) Tj T*
(of both old and new shares by the total number of shares, and deduct) Tj T*
(the average price from the market quotations. This gives the selling) Tj T*
(value of the rights.?--From ?American Railways as Investments,? by Carl) Tj T*
(Snyder.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
