(?It will be seen from the above that a 20% decline in the net earnings) Tj T*
(would, in the first instance, mean a decrease of 80% in the surplus;) Tj T*
(while in the second case, the same decline would mean a decrease of) Tj T*
(only 26% in the surplus--figures which sufficiently indicate what a) Tj T*
(high percentage of fixed charges means.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?In this connection it may be further noted that in the large holding) Tj T*
(companies, like the Pennsylvania, the New York Central, the Union) Tj T*
(Pacific, and others, the factor of safety and the surplus shown tends) Tj T*
(to be relatively more stable than in companies largely or exclusively) Tj T*
(dependent upon the earnings of their own roads. This is due to the) Tj T*
(general custom of American Railways of paying out in dividends only a) Tj T*
(part of the actual surplus earned. From this it results that dividends) Tj T*
(are much more stable than earnings, and that the income of the) Tj T*
(holding companies from this source will correspondingly show smaller) Tj T*
(fluctuations than earnings. When, therefore, as in the case of some) Tj T*
(of the large holding companies named, the income from investments) Tj T*
(represents a considerable portion of the total net income shown, the) Tj T*
(surplus, other things being equal, will be much more stable than in) Tj T*
(other companies.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?It is needless to add that this stability is still further heightened) Tj T*
(when, as in the case of the Pennsylvania, Union Pacific and some other) Tj T*
(roads, the percentage of fixed charges is at the same time low.?--From) Tj T*
(?American Railways as Investments,? by Carl Snyder.) Tj T*
