(In reviewing the movements of prices from 1896 to 1905, the most) Tj T*
(important question is, what caused the reversal of form in 1903? A) Tj T*
(complete answer to this question would be highly educational. There) Tj T*
(was no panic, nothing faintly resembling one; business suffered some) Tj T*
(stagnation, it is true; there was a falling off in the iron and steel) Tj T*
(business, but crops were good, and wheat, corn, oats, and cotton) Tj T*
(brought good prices in both 1903 and 1904. Serious business depression) Tj T*
(was more in anticipation than in realization, but 1904 witnessed no) Tj T*
(material recovery in prices. These causes do not fully explain so) Tj T*
(radical a change. If conditions had been such as to cause a reduction) Tj T*
(of dividends, or a scarcity of money in 1903, the decline would) Tj T*
(be explained, but money was plentiful enough, and dividends were) Tj T*
(unchanged. The ratio of dividends as compared with prices was also) Tj T*
(fairly maintained from 1896 to 1902, and it would appear that prices) Tj T*
(should merely stop advancing when dividends became stationary; but) Tj T*
(prices did not merely stand still, they went materially backward.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Without pretending to enter into a full discussion of the causes for) Tj T*
(the change, one or two points may assist in forming a conclusion. The) Tj T*
(steady advance in prices from 1896 to 1902 represented two things--a) Tj T*
(recovery from the great depression of 1893, and the natural advances) Tj T*
(of property values in a prosperous and growing country. The latter) Tj T*
(point is the more important, and as there has been no cessation of the) Tj T*
(growth of population or prosperity, other causes for the reversion) Tj T*
(must be sought. It is not sufficient to merely say that the recovery) Tj T*
(over-leaped itself, for such an event would have plainly mirrored) Tj T*
(itself in a reduction in the rate of dividend returns.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Capitalization of railroads in 1903 increased about 14% as compared) Tj T*
(with an average increase of 6% in the preceding seven years. Add to) Tj T*
(this the tremendous increase in the capitalization of industrial) Tj T*
(corporations, and an over-supply of stocks appears as one of the) Tj T*
(contributary causes--undigested securities.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Dividend rates were maintained, but were not increased. This) Tj T*
(particularly affects the simon pure speculator. Nothing will drive him) Tj T*
(into a panic quicker than a decreased dividend, and nothing makes him) Tj T*
(so sanguine of higher prices as an increase in the rate of payment. He) Tj T*
(is always basing his operations on rumors of higher dividends, and when) Tj T*
(one of these rumors fails of verification, it is almost as bad as a) Tj T*
(decrease.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(And dividends did decrease in one important quarter; United States) Tj T*
(Steel, the speculative favorite, capitalized more heavily than a) Tj T*
(dozen ordinary corporations, cut its rate from 4 to 3½%, with every) Tj T*
(promise of a further reduction. This had a far reaching effect, both on) Tj T*
(speculators and small investors.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is certain that fundamental conditions have more to do in shaping) Tj T*
(prices than has speculation, but the speculator helps, and in 1903) Tj T*
