(experience and information you can from history, but _speculate_ on the) Tj T*
(_future_.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The man who enters the market with insufficient capital, who expects) Tj T*
(to get rich quick or who allows success to lead him into excesses and) Tj T*
(over-speculation will lose. It is as certain as death. He may succeed) Tj T*
(for a time but not for long.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Operations based on ?tips? or ?charts? will lead to final disaster.) Tj T*
(This thing of trying to attribute habits to a market is, in the) Tj T*
(writer?s opinion, ridiculous. The movements of prices are caused) Tj T*
(by events. We know that in periods of financial stringency or) Tj T*
(business depression prices fall, and in periods of inflation and) Tj T*
(good times prices rise. It is possible to formulate a system founded) Tj T*
(on repetitions applicable to every game of chance, and laid out on) Tj T*
(paper, that system will appear infallible. You can find the exponents) Tj T*
(of machine-made riches in every pool-room and gambling house in the) Tj T*
(country. They all eventually lose and there is nothing to show that) Tj T*
(the advocates of charts as a basis for stock trading ever fared any) Tj T*
(better. Imagine James R. Keene, or J. P. Morgan poring over a chart and) Tj T*
(operating thereon. Even if the market did have habits, as soon as these) Tj T*
(habits were recognized and followed by a sufficient number of people,) Tj T*
(the technical position would be rendered so rotten that the charts) Tj T*
(would fail from that influence alone. Charts, employed as a convenient) Tj T*
(method of picturing the past, may have a certain value, but used as) Tj T*
(a basis for trading they are an evidence of laziness or incapacity,) Tj T*
(or both. It requires hard work to be successful in any line. Thinking) Tj T*
(is hard work, study is hard work, research is hard work; and it is) Tj T*
(infinitely easier to bet on repetitions all nicely laid out in crooked) Tj T*
(lines on a sheet of paper than to laboriously erect sound deductions;) Tj T*
(but the difference in the two methods is that one will succeed and the) Tj T*
(other will fail.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We may also resort to the ultimatum employed by those eminent) Tj T*
(citizens who punch each other?s noses in a prize-ring, i.e., tell the) Tj T*
(chart-players to ?go and get a reputation.? When they can show even one) Tj T*
(instance of a fortune accumulated by this method their cause will be) Tj T*
(greatly strengthened.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the exception taken to Mr. Dow?s theory of $2,500 being the minimum) Tj T*
(amount necessary for operations in small lots, nothing could be further) Tj T*
(from the present writer?s intentions than to recommend transactions on) Tj T*
(insufficient margin. What is sought is merely the truth. The contention) Tj T*
(that it is wise to stimulate extreme conservatism will not hold. If the) Tj T*
(naming of a certain sum far in excess of real needs is praiseworthy, we) Tj T*
(may expand the matter indefinitely and name $5,000 or even $10,000, as) Tj T*
(the limit.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But on the other hand, errors on the side of prudence are vastly) Tj T*
(preferable to errors on the side of rashness. In this as in all other) Tj T*
(things, the golden mean is the really desirable factor.) Tj T*
